---
import LegalLayout from '../layouts/LegalLayout.astro'
import { site } from '../config/site'
import '../styles/doc.css'
---

<!-- Trust page. Claims must match how the game actually runs: server-rolled
     dice and server-validated moves in online play, engine-validated local
     play, AI opponents disclosed. Do not overstate. -->
<LegalLayout
  title={`Fair Play — How Dice and Matchmaking Work in ${site.name}`}
  description={`How ${site.name} keeps matches fair: dice rolled on our servers, every move checked against the rules, matches recorded, and AI opponents clearly labelled.`}
  canonical={`${site.url}/fair-play`}
>
  <h1>Fair Play</h1>
  <p class="doc-meta">
    Every backgammon player has, at some point, been sure the dice were against
    them. Here is exactly how ours work.
  </p>

  <h2>Your phone does not roll the dice</h2>
  <p>
    In an online match, neither player's phone decides anything. Your device
    asks our server for a roll, the server generates it and stores it with the
    match, and both players are told the same numbers at the same moment. A
    modified app cannot pick its own numbers, because the numbers were never
    its to pick.
  </p>

  <h2>The dice do not know who you are</h2>
  <p>
    Rolls are random and independent. They are not weighted by your balance,
    your level, your win streak, your opponent, or whether you just bought
    anything. There is no code that makes you lose to keep a match close, and
    there is none that rewards a purchase with better numbers. The game has
    nothing to gain from it: your chips are virtual and stay in the app.
  </p>

  <h2>Illegal moves are impossible, not discouraged</h2>
  <p>
    The rules of backgammon live in one piece of code that both your app and
    our server use. Before a move counts, it is checked against every legal
    move for that roll. If it is not in the list, it does not happen. That is
    also why the board only ever highlights moves you are allowed to make.
  </p>

  <h2>Matches are recorded</h2>
  <p>
    Every roll and every move of a match is stored. If a game ends in a way you
    did not expect, we can replay it exactly as it happened and tell you what
    the server saw. Write to <a href="/support">Support</a> with roughly when
    it happened and we will look.
  </p>

  <h2>When your opponent is an AI</h2>
  <p>
    Matchmaking prefers a human. When no one is available at your tier, you are
    paired with an AI opponent so you are not left waiting. The AI plays the
    same board with the same rules and the same server-rolled dice as a person
    would — it does not see your dice in advance and it does not get extra
    ones. Difficulty comes from how well it picks moves, nothing else.
  </p>

  <h2>Cheating and collusion</h2>
  <p>
    Because the server owns the dice, the moves, and the score, the usual
    cheats simply have nothing to attack. What we do act on is human behaviour:
    stalling, dumping matches to move chips between accounts, or running
    multiple accounts. Our <a href="/community-rules">Community Rules</a>
    explain what happens then.
  </p>

  <h2>Found something that looks wrong?</h2>
  <p>
    Tell us. A time, a screenshot, and your display name are enough for us to
    find the match. We would rather check a hundred honest complaints than
    leave one real bug running.
  </p>
</LegalLayout>
